                    92.0,
                    914.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "To analyze, assess, and revise your introduction, do this:",
            "block_bbox": [
                92,
                929,
                571,
                953
            ],
            "block_id": 15,
            "block_order": 14,
            "group_id": 15,
            "global_block_id": 15,
            "global_group_id": 15,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    92.0,
                    929.0
                ],
                [
                    571.0,
                    929.0
                ],
                [
                    571.0,
                    953.0
                ],
                [
                    92.0,
                    953.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "1. Determine whether you are posing a practical or conceptual problem. Do you want readers to do something or to think something?",
            "block_bbox": [
                92,
                967,
                782,
                1017
            ],
            "block_id": 16,
            "block_order": 15,
            "group_id": 16,
            "global_block_id": 16,
            "global_group_id": 16,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    92.0,
                    967.0
                ],
                [
                    782.0,
                    967.0
                ],
                [
                    782.0,
                    1017.0
                ],
                [
                    92.0,
                    1017.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "2. Draw a line after your introduction. If you cannot quickly locate the end of your introduction, neither will your readers, who might then miss both your problem and its solution, the main point of your paper.",
            "block_bbox": [
                91,
                1025,
                784,
                1102
            ],
            "block_id": 17,
            "block_order": 16,
            "group_id": 17,
            "global_block_id": 17,
            "global_group_id": 17,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    91.0,
                    1025.0
                ],
                [
                    784.0,
                    1025.0
                ],
                [
                    784.0,
                    1102.0
                ],
                [
                    91.0,
                    1102.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "3. Divide the introduction into its three parts: shared context + problem + solution/main point/claim. If you cannot quickly make those divisions, your introduction is likely to seem unfocused.",
            "block_bbox": [
                91,
                1109,
                784,
                1184
            ],
            "block_id": 18,
            "block_order": 17,
            "group_id": 18,
            "global_block_id": 18,
            "global_group_id": 18,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    91.0,
                    1109.0
                ],
                [
                    784.0,
                    1109.0
                ],
                [
                    784.0,
                    1184.0
                ],
                [
                    91.0,
                    1184.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "4. Make sure the first word of the first sentence after the shared context is but, however, or some other word indicating that you will challenge that shared",
            "block_bbox": [
